;;; (theourgia platform-numbers) -- every number the FFI reads that a
;;; platform decides, as MEASURED data, one row per platform.
;;;
;;; KEY: A ROW IS A READING, NOT A TRANSCRIPTION. Each row below is the
;;; output of test/probe/layout.c on that platform, byte for byte as the
;;; probe printed it (the archived files are under test/probe/readings/,
;;; and a row of the suite compares each row with its file). The code
;;; asks this library for a number by name -- (platform-number 'O_APPEND),
;;; (platform-field 'stat 'st_mode 'offset) -- and never writes one down:
;;; a number written into the code is a claim about one platform that
;;; reads as true on all of them, and the first Linux run found seven such
;;; claims false, one of which wrote every log record over the start of
;;; its segment.
;;;
;;; THE ROW IS SELECTED ONCE, WHEN THIS LIBRARY IS INITIALISED, from what
;;; Chez gives with no foreign call: (machine-type) names the system and
;;; the architecture, and on Linux the C library is read from the objects
;;; this process has mapped (/proc/self/maps), glibc and musl each
;;; identified by name. ffi.sc imports this library, so the selection
;;; happens before ffi.sc's body makes its first foreign call.
;;;
;;; NEVER: A PLATFORM WITHOUT A ROW IS REFUSED, NOT SERVED A NEIGHBOUR'S.
;;; A neighbouring row is the failure this library exists to end -- the
;;; right offsets for another machine. So an unlisted platform (another
;;; architecture, macOS on x86_64 or under Rosetta, a Linux with musl, a
;;; process that cannot read /proc/self/maps) writes
;;;   (error platform-unmeasured (system ...) (machine ...) (remedy ...))
;;; on the standard error and exits 75, before anything else runs. It
;;; cannot answer the ordinary way: nothing that renders an answer has
;;; been loaded, and the standard output of the MCP shell is its protocol
;;; stream. The remedy is the probe: run test/probe/layout.c there and add
;;; its output as a row.
;;;
;;; NOTE: A NUMBER A PLATFORM DOES NOT HAVE IS `absent` IN ITS ROW, and
;;; asking for it is a programming error (an assertion), not a refusal:
;;; the code reads such a number only inside the branch for the platform
;;; that has it (F_FULLFSYNC on macOS, the sysctl names on FreeBSD).
;;;
;;; This library imports nothing of the tree: it is below every one of
;;; them.

(library (theourgia platform-numbers)
  (export platform-number platform-field platform-field-present?
          platform-struct-size platform-struct-value platform-type-size
          platform-wait-sample platform-key platform-row platform-readings
          reading-key maps-libc)
  (import (chezscheme))

